Hi there!

> 1) When I calculate the standard dev attributable to a random effect
> (using inla.contrib.sd <http://inla.contrib.sd>()) the estimates are similar to those from lme
> when the Maximum Likelihood method is used, and are shrunk relative to
> an lme fit with REML.  Is it possible to obtain estimates from an inla
> fit that are more in line with those produced by using REML in lme?

Be aware that the mode can be different from the mean when the marginals
are skewed and these components has a tendency to be very skewed with
few observations, and also you have the effect of the prior.

I think you can get (closer to REML) by require that the 'random
effects' sum to zero, like adding constr=TRUE to the appropriate f()'s.

>
> 2) How can nested random effects be specified?

>

> ##What about a nested model?  We nest Machine inside of Worker to
> estimate the
>    #variance of workers on different machines
> lme2 <- lme(score ~ Machine,random=~1|
> Worker/Machine,data=m,method="ML")

sorry, I don't know what that formula means in terms of the model. can
you write down the model in equations?

thanks
H

